00:01:48,660 --> 00:01:54,320
we can summarize for you with this diagram here,

37
00:01:54,320 --> 00:01:54,660
right?

38
00:01:54,660 --> 00:01:57,260
What's on this diagram?

39
00:01:57,900 --> 00:01:59,360
If I asked you to draw it,

40
00:01:59,360 --> 00:02:00,140
that is, a computer —

41
00:02:00,140 --> 00:02:00,960
If you want to draw what a computer

42
00:02:00,960 --> 00:02:01,960
is made up of,

43
00:02:01,960 --> 00:02:03,020
I don't know if everyone

44
00:02:03,020 --> 00:02:05,140
can roughly sketch this diagram yourselves.

45
00:02:05,140 --> 00:02:09,470
On this diagram,

46
00:02:09,470 --> 00:02:10,190
this...

47
00:02:10,190 --> 00:02:12,290
what is this?

48
00:02:12,290 --> 00:02:15,620
Right?

49
00:02:15,620 --> 00:02:17,220
This, we call it the bus.

50
00:02:17,220 --> 00:02:17,580
Agreed?

51
00:02:17,580 --> 00:02:18,700
That is to say,

52
00:02:18,700 --> 00:02:20,660
all those things inside our computer,

53
00:02:20,660 --> 00:02:21,420
all of them,

54
00:02:21,420 --> 00:02:22,540
the other things are called components.

55
00:02:22,540 --> 00:02:23,240
You can think of them as components.

56
00:02:23,240 --> 00:02:24,080
Now these components,

57
00:02:24,080 --> 00:02:25,000
they're all connected through a single bus,

58
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:26,220
wired together,

59
00:02:26,220 --> 00:02:27,960
just like a highway.

60
00:02:27,960 --> 00:02:30,360
So this connecting part,

61
00:02:30,360 --> 00:02:31,040
there are several big blocks

62
00:02:31,040 --> 00:02:32,700
Now, the biggest block of this thing

63
00:02:32,700 --> 00:02:33,920
needless to say

64
00:02:33,920 --> 00:02:36,460
this is the core of our computer, and what is it called?

65
00:02:36,460 --> 00:02:38,500
it's the CPU

66
00:02:38,500 --> 00:02:40,960
Now, the CPU is actually...

67
00:02:40,960 --> 00:02:43,680
If I ask you right now, what is the CPU?

68
00:02:43,680 --> 00:02:44,540
you should still be able to say it

69
00:02:44,540 --> 00:02:48,500
it's the Central Processing Unit

70
00:02:48,500 --> 00:02:50,240
What does CPU mean?

71
00:02:50,240 --> 00:02:53,980
Translated into Chinese, it's called the central processing unit

72
00:02:53,980 --> 00:02:54,780
right?

73
00:02:54,780 --> 00:02:56,760
If you go out there

74
00:02:56,760 --> 00:02:58,340
say, to a job interview

75
00:02:58,340 --> 00:02:59,940
do you think anyone would ask about these things?

76
00:02:59,940 --> 00:03:00,780
Maybe they will

77
00:03:00,780 --> 00:03:02,680
After you've learned these things in school

78
00:03:02,680 --> 00:03:03,140
and you go out

79
00:03:03,140 --> 00:03:04,700
it'd be good if you can explain them yourselves

80
00:03:04,700 --> 00:03:06,760
Now, this

81
00:03:06,760 --> 00:03:10,860
What's actually inside this CPU?

82
00:03:10,860 --> 00:03:14,660
When you took computer organization,

83
00:03:14,660 --> 00:03:15,520
did your instructor cover this?

84
00:03:15,520 --> 00:03:17,520
Or on your own,

85
00:03:17,520 --> 00:03:20,860
what's your understanding of this part?

86
00:03:20,860 --> 00:03:23,760
Let me quickly walk you through it.

87
00:03:23,760 --> 00:03:25,060
On our side here,

88
00:03:25,060 --> 00:03:25,720
there are these

89
00:03:25,720 --> 00:03:26,740
Inside this CPU,

90
00:03:26,740 --> 00:03:28,040
there are these main blocks.

91
00:03:28,040 --> 00:03:29,180
The first one is called

92
00:03:29,180 --> 00:03:31,160
abbreviated as CU.

93
00:03:31,160 --> 00:03:33,020
Once you're out in the industry, you'll know

94
00:03:33,020 --> 00:03:34,140
what CU means.

95
00:03:34,140 --> 00:03:35,700
It's the control unit,

96
00:03:35,700 --> 00:03:37,020
the control unit.

97
00:03:37,020 --> 00:03:38,440
Understand?

98
00:03:38,440 --> 00:03:40,800
The control unit is essentially the control part.

99
00:03:40,800 --> 00:03:43,540
To put its main job simply,

100
00:03:43,540 --> 00:03:45,020
and this is something we'll cover later,

101
00:03:45,020 --> 00:03:47,500
it's basically feeding some stuff into an instruction

102
00:03:47,500 --> 00:03:49,200
parsing it into something

103
00:03:49,200 --> 00:03:50,500
processing it, running it, right?

104
00:03:50,500 --> 00:03:52,740
the control unit is over here

105
00:03:52,740 --> 00:03:53,720
now

106
00:03:53,720 --> 00:03:57,440
what is this one called?

107
00:03:57,440 --> 00:04:01,700
we have an abbreviation for it, ALU

108
00:04:01,700 --> 00:04:04,480
that stands for the arithmetic logic unit

109
00:04:04,480 --> 00:04:06,040
now for the actual computation

110
00:04:06,040 --> 00:04:06,960
our computer

111
00:04:06,960 --> 00:04:08,100
a computer is meant for computing

112
00:04:08,100 --> 00:04:08,480
to put it plainly

113
00:04:08,480 --> 00:04:09,960
it's not meant for playing games

114
00:04:09,960 --> 00:04:11,580
so a computer

115
00:04:11,580 --> 00:04:13,220
when it was first invented

116
00:04:13,220 --> 00:04:13,920
it was for computing

117
00:04:13,920 --> 00:04:15,060
so where does that computing happen?

118
00:04:15,060 --> 00:04:15,920
which part does it happen in?

119
00:04:15,920 --> 00:04:16,940
actually right here

120
00:04:16,940 --> 00:04:18,100
the arithmetic logic unit

121
00:04:18,100 --> 00:04:18,800
You can tell right away,

122
00:04:18,800 --> 00:04:19,880
the computations we do

123
00:04:19,880 --> 00:04:21,080
are nothing more than arithmetic operations

124
00:04:21,080 --> 00:04:21,540
and what else?

125
00:04:21,540 --> 00:04:22,880
Logical operations,

126
00:04:22,880 --> 00:04:23,460
right?

127
00:04:23,460 --> 00:04:25,240
When you learned C,

128
00:04:25,240 --> 00:04:26,540
that addition, subtraction, multiplication, and division,

129
00:04:26,540 --> 00:04:27,100
what is that?

130
00:04:27,100 --> 00:04:28,840
That falls under arithmetic operations.

131
00:04:28,840 --> 00:04:30,780
What else falls under logical operations?

132
00:04:30,780 --> 00:04:33,540
AND, OR, NOT, things like that.

133
00:04:33,540 --> 00:04:34,660
Think about it, isn't that so?

134
00:04:34,660 --> 00:04:37,160
So when you boil everything down to the end,

135
00:04:37,160 --> 00:04:38,960
it's nothing more than arithmetic operations and logical operations.

136
00:04:38,960 --> 00:04:40,700
So the arithmetic logic unit,

137
00:04:40,700 --> 00:04:41,920
that is right here.

138
00:04:41,920 --> 00:04:44,160
And then there's...

139
00:04:44,160 --> 00:04:45,480
Besides that,

140
00:04:45,480 --> 00:04:46,500
there is another very important part.

141
00:04:46,500 --> 00:04:47,280
It's basically a bunch of things

142
00:04:47,280 --> 00:04:49,360
not just a single thing

143
00:04:49,360 --> 00:04:50,360
It's a whole collection of things

144
00:04:50,360 --> 00:04:50,960
And this collection,

145
00:04:50,960 --> 00:04:52,640
we call them all registers

146
00:04:52,640 --> 00:04:55,500
In other words, the data you need to process

147
00:04:55,500 --> 00:04:56,500
is actually all in this,

148
00:04:56,500 --> 00:04:58,260
all stored in these registers

149
00:04:58,260 --> 00:04:58,920
and then,

150
00:04:58,920 --> 00:05:01,480
it's handed over to the arithmetic logic unit to perform the computation

151
00:05:01,480 --> 00:05:05,000
So that's what a processor is

152
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:06,620
Now, besides that,

153
00:05:06,620 --> 00:05:08,780
we'll take a closer look at it later

154
00:05:08,780 --> 00:05:09,980
Now let's look at the others

155
00:05:09,980 --> 00:05:12,260
This one here is called the IO bridge

156
00:05:12,260 --> 00:05:13,100
In practice, it's

157
00:05:13,100 --> 00:05:17,160
a bridge for our processor to interact with the outside world

158
00:05:17,160 --> 00:05:17,900
a bridge

159
00:05:17,900 --> 00:05:19,620
And this bridge,

160
00:05:19,620 --> 00:05:22,540
along with the bus, and one other very important component

161
00:05:22,540 --> 00:05:24,660
That's our main memory.

162
00:05:24,660 --> 00:05:26,380
That's the memory where our data is stored.

163
00:05:26,380 --> 00:05:28,240
All connected together.

164
00:05:28,240 --> 00:05:31,420
Now, below this bus,

165
00:05:31,420 --> 00:05:32,960
there are a large number of

166
00:05:32,960 --> 00:05:34,960
what we actually call peripherals.

167
00:05:34,960 --> 00:05:36,080
Because with just these,

168
00:05:36,080 --> 00:05:37,880
with just a CPU and memory,

169
00:05:37,880 --> 00:05:39,180
the computer

170
00:05:39,180 --> 00:05:40,300
is basically unusable to us,

171
00:05:40,300 --> 00:05:40,780
right?

172
00:05:40,780 --> 00:05:42,680
So we need some peripherals,

173
00:05:42,680 --> 00:05:43,880
like a keyboard and mouse,

174
00:05:43,880 --> 00:05:45,500
and a display,

175
00:05:45,500 --> 00:05:46,780
so we can interact with it.

176
00:05:46,780 --> 00:05:49,280
So everything down here is peripherals.

177
00:05:49,280 --> 00:05:51,680
And that's what makes up a computer.

178
00:05:51,680 --> 00:05:53,940
Of course, this is a classic diagram.

179
00:05:53,940 --> 00:05:57,820
It's basically the same as our PCs, desktops, and laptops.

180
00:05:57,820 --> 00:06:01,340
And some phones have a similar organizational architecture.

181
00:06:01,340 --> 00:06:02,760
It might be slightly different from it,

182
00:06:02,760 --> 00:06:05,120
but overall it's pretty much like this.

183
00:06:05,120 --> 00:06:05,880
A computer is

184
00:06:05,880 --> 00:06:09,640
just this kind of thing.

185
00:06:09,640 --> 00:06:11,920
If you want to reinforce this impression later,

186
00:06:11,920 --> 00:06:14,380
you can go home and try drawing this diagram yourself.

187
00:06:14,380 --> 00:06:16,380
Because some things, when described in words,

188
00:06:16,380 --> 00:06:17,580
might not be easy to remember,

189
00:06:17,580 --> 00:06:19,300
while graphical things tend to be easier to remember.

190
00:06:19,300 --> 00:06:22,880
So in our course, we draw a lot of diagrams.

191
00:06:22,880 --> 00:06:24,440
Through these diagrams, you can

192
00:06:24,440 --> 00:06:26,420
deepen your learning and mastery of the knowledge.

193
00:06:26,420 --> 00:06:31,770
Now, this computer we're talking about,

194
00:06:31,770 --> 00:06:32,990
the one in the diagram above,

195
00:06:32,990 --> 00:06:34,690
this computer thing,

196
00:06:34,690 --> 00:06:37,010
we actually often call it the

197
00:06:37,010 --> 00:06:38,210
von Neumann architecture.

198
00:06:38,210 --> 00:06:41,050
When you learned computer organization and design,

199
00:06:41,050 --> 00:06:42,010
you probably still remember this concept, right?

200
00:06:42,010 --> 00:06:43,930
That is, the von Neumann...

201
00:06:43,930 --> 00:06:46,170
This kind of organization

202
00:06:46,170 --> 00:06:48,030
Now, the other corresponding approach is called what?

203
00:06:48,030 --> 00:06:48,850
Do you all remember?

204
00:06:48,850 --> 00:06:51,510
It's called the Harvard architecture.

205
00:06:51,510 --> 00:06:52,890
Right?

206
00:06:52,890 --> 00:06:55,690
So von Neumann and Harvard are the two

207
00:06:55,690 --> 00:06:57,590
major architectures in our computers.

208
00:06:57,590 --> 00:06:59,310
That is, two ways of organizing the hardware.

209
00:06:59,310 --> 00:07:02,410
Now, what's the difference between these two architectures?

210
00:07:02,410 --> 00:07:05,790
From this diagram, we can

211
00:07:05,790 --> 00:07:08,450
see what the difference is.

212
00:07:08,450 --> 00:07:13,100
For the von Neumann architecture,

213
00:07:13,100 --> 00:07:15,380
you see it only has one bus to the outside.

214
00:07:15,380 --> 00:07:16,800
This CPU here

215
00:07:16,800 --> 00:07:19,700
only communicates with the outside through a single bus.

216
00:07:19,700 --> 00:07:20,680
Everyone see that?

217
00:07:20,680 --> 00:07:23,080
And the Harvard architecture,

218
00:07:23,080 --> 00:07:24,960
what's its obvious feature?

219
00:07:24,960 --> 00:07:27,600
It has two buses to communicate with the outside.

220
00:07:27,600 --> 00:07:28,400
Right?

221
00:07:28,400 --> 00:07:29,600
So the Harvard

222
00:07:29,600 --> 00:07:30,440
Harvard architecture,

223
00:07:30,440 --> 00:07:33,920
it separates instructions and data

224
00:07:33,920 --> 00:07:37,060
meaning the instructions it runs and the data it operates on

225
00:07:37,060 --> 00:07:37,740
are separated,

226
00:07:37,740 --> 00:07:39,220
kept in two memory regions,

227
00:07:39,220 --> 00:07:42,360
and then they interact through two buses.

228
00:07:42,360 --> 00:07:45,160
Whereas the von Neumann,

229
00:07:45,160 --> 00:07:46,980
relatively speaking, you can actually understand it as

230
00:07:46,980 --> 00:07:48,840
its storage area

231
00:07:48,840 --> 00:07:51,640
contains both instructions and data.

232
00:07:51,640 --> 00:07:52,500
Does everyone understand?

233
00:07:52,500 --> 00:07:54,000
That's how it works.

234
00:07:54,000 --> 00:07:55,720
That's the most important difference between them.

235
00:07:55,720 --> 00:07:56,320
So, this,

236
00:07:56,320 --> 00:08:00,140
we can say

237
00:08:00,140 --> 00:08:02,520
in fact, a large number of PCs, servers,

238
00:08:02,520 --> 00:08:03,220
and so on,

239
00:08:03,220 --> 00:08:04,180
are actually based on

240
00:08:04,180 --> 00:08:05,340
the von Neumann architecture

241
00:08:05,340 --> 00:08:07,040
the main ones.

242
00:08:07,040 --> 00:08:07,940
So going forward,

243
00:08:07,940 --> 00:08:09,560
we'll actually focus on this von Neumann architecture

244
00:08:09,560 --> 00:08:10,920
for our introduction.

245
00:08:10,920 --> 00:08:12,220
Now, this

246
00:08:12,220 --> 00:08:14,300
approach it uses,

247
00:08:14,300 --> 00:08:15,260
its main advantage,

248
00:08:15,260 --> 00:08:16,060
as you know,

249
00:08:16,060 --> 00:08:17,560
is that it only has one

250
00:08:17,560 --> 00:08:18,940
-- you only need to design one bus.

251
00:08:18,940 --> 00:08:19,840
So in this regard,

252
00:08:19,840 --> 00:08:20,780
as you can imagine,

253
00:08:20,780 --> 00:08:23,040
compared to the Harvard architecture,

254
00:08:23,040 --> 00:08:25,040
its hardware design is definitely simpler.

255
00:08:25,040 --> 00:08:25,800
Right?

256
00:08:25,800 --> 00:08:28,200
The one on the right here looks a bit more complex,

257
00:08:28,200 --> 00:08:28,500
doesn't it?

258
00:08:28,500 --> 00:08:31,960
So this simplicity,

259
00:08:31,960 --> 00:08:33,980
but after that simplicity,

260
00:08:33,980 --> 00:08:36,380
the overhead of its bus

261
00:08:36,380 --> 00:08:37,740
it tends to be a bit smaller

262
00:08:37,740 --> 00:08:39,800
the control logic is simpler to implement

263
00:08:39,800 --> 00:08:41,760
basically, the hardware unit design is simpler

264
00:08:41,760 --> 00:08:43,060
as for the Harvard architecture,

265
00:08:43,060 --> 00:08:45,040
its hardware design is more complex

266
00:08:45,040 --> 00:08:46,280
but once that complexity is there,

267
00:08:46,280 --> 00:08:47,940
what you get in return is higher execution efficiency

268
00:08:47,940 --> 00:08:50,480
because the data and the instructions

269
00:08:50,480 --> 00:08:51,920
don't have to compete for the same bus

270
00:08:51,920 --> 00:08:54,420
with two separate paths, it's naturally going to run a bit faster

271
00:08:54,420 --> 00:08:54,980
think about it, isn't that right?

272
00:08:54,980 --> 00:08:59,400
So on some small microcontrollers,

273
00:08:59,400 --> 00:09:00,960
on tiny processors,

274
00:09:00,960 --> 00:09:02,180
the preference is to use the Harvard architecture

275
00:09:02,180 --> 00:09:05,220
whereas large servers and these

276
00:09:05,220 --> 00:09:07,660
things like these desktop machines,

277
00:09:07,660 --> 00:09:09,280
compared to these small controllers,

278
00:09:09,280 --> 00:09:10,600
they are already relatively large processors

279
00:09:10,600 --> 00:09:12,600
so they use the von Neumann architecture,

280
00:09:12,600 --> 00:09:14,320
but it's not absolute

281
00:09:14,320 --> 00:09:15,700
That is to say, these two architectures

282
00:09:15,700 --> 00:09:16,940
also see a certain amount of convergence

283
00:09:16,940 --> 00:09:18,440
in today's designs.

284
00:09:18,440 --> 00:09:21,500
Now, in the rest of this course,

285
00:09:21,500 --> 00:09:24,020
we'll mainly focus on the von Neumann architecture

286
00:09:24,020 --> 00:09:24,900
for our introduction.

