00:09:33,300 --> 00:09:35,120
because we can't possibly lay it all out in full.

294
00:09:35,120 --> 00:09:37,000
So everyone should just know that this is how it works

295
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:41,480
That's the second part of the hardware components,

296
00:09:41,480 --> 00:09:43,140
which I've just briefly introduced to you.

297
00:09:43,140 --> 00:09:44,200
All right.

298
00:09:44,200 --> 00:09:46,040
Now, next,

299
00:09:46,040 --> 00:09:49,820
we'll move on to the second block of this part,

300
00:09:49,820 --> 00:09:53,140
which is about program storage and execution.

301
00:09:53,140 --> 00:09:57,240
So actually,

302
00:09:57,240 --> 00:09:58,280
that is to say, we,

303
00:09:58,280 --> 00:10:00,780
as we talked about at the very beginning,

304
00:10:00,780 --> 00:10:01,800
a hello world,

305
00:10:01,800 --> 00:10:03,640
once you've written a program,

306
00:10:03,640 --> 00:10:05,640
how does it actually get running in the end,

307
00:10:05,640 --> 00:10:07,520
and how does a

308
00:10:07,520 --> 00:10:09,000
hello world get printed on the screen?

309
00:10:09,000 --> 00:10:09,480
Right?

310
00:10:09,480 --> 00:10:11,320
So what we're really going to do

311
00:10:11,320 --> 00:10:12,280
is take this part

312
00:10:12,280 --> 00:10:13,720
and tie it all together and walk through it again.

313
00:10:13,720 --> 00:10:15,400
So,

314
00:10:15,400 --> 00:10:16,460
first, this diagram,

315
00:10:16,460 --> 00:10:17,560
it's still the same diagram from before,

316
00:10:17,560 --> 00:10:18,400
it's a diagram of

317
00:10:18,400 --> 00:10:20,760
how a computer is put together, placed here.

318
00:10:20,760 --> 00:10:21,980
So we,

319
00:10:21,980 --> 00:10:24,400
let's suppose we've now written a hello.c,

320
00:10:24,400 --> 00:10:27,020
so once this C source code is written,

321
00:10:27,020 --> 00:10:28,340
Can we just run it directly?

322
00:10:28,340 --> 00:10:29,700
Of course not.

323
00:10:29,700 --> 00:10:30,020
Right?

324
00:10:30,020 --> 00:10:32,960
What do we have to do with it first?

325
00:10:32,960 --> 00:10:36,160
First we have to take it and

326
00:10:36,160 --> 00:10:38,900
generate an executable program

327
00:10:38,900 --> 00:10:39,140
Right?

328
00:10:39,140 --> 00:10:40,320
On Linux,

329
00:10:40,320 --> 00:10:41,280
it's called a.out

330
00:10:41,280 --> 00:10:42,520
By default it's called a.out

331
00:10:42,520 --> 00:10:44,220
Now what do we call this process?

332
00:10:44,220 --> 00:10:45,960
It's called compilation and linking

333
00:10:45,960 --> 00:10:46,640
Right?

334
00:10:46,640 --> 00:10:49,360
We compile and link this thing,

335
00:10:49,360 --> 00:10:51,440
and once we've generated an a.out,

336
00:10:51,440 --> 00:10:52,960
we know this a.out,

337
00:10:52,960 --> 00:10:54,540
this thing,

338
00:10:54,540 --> 00:10:56,440
where is it placed now?

339
00:10:56,440 --> 00:10:58,740
It's placed,

340
00:10:58,740 --> 00:11:00,940
we'd say, on the hard disk.

341
00:11:00,940 --> 00:11:01,120
Right?

342
00:11:01,120 --> 00:11:02,720
So the stuff on the hard disk

343
00:11:02,720 --> 00:11:04,100
does it just run directly?

344
00:11:04,100 --> 00:11:05,660
Not quite.

345
00:11:05,660 --> 00:11:07,200
We say that when we're

346
00:11:07,200 --> 00:11:08,760
say you're in the share directory

347
00:11:08,760 --> 00:11:09,780
and you type in a.out,

348
00:11:09,780 --> 00:11:10,560
after you hit Enter,

349
00:11:10,560 --> 00:11:11,500
what happens?

350
00:11:11,500 --> 00:11:14,560
Well, this a.out

351
00:11:14,560 --> 00:11:16,140
this program,

352
00:11:16,140 --> 00:11:16,980
we need to first

353
00:11:16,980 --> 00:11:18,020
load it into where?

354
00:11:18,020 --> 00:11:19,820
Don't we need to first

355
00:11:19,820 --> 00:11:21,440
load it into memory?

356
00:11:21,440 --> 00:11:21,700
Right?

357
00:11:21,700 --> 00:11:24,680
Now, this a.out,

358
00:11:24,680 --> 00:11:27,380
it's an executable program.

359
00:11:27,380 --> 00:11:28,160
Now, an executable program,

360
00:11:28,160 --> 00:11:29,540
if you open it up,

361
00:11:29,540 --> 00:11:30,780
No matter which text editor you use

362
00:11:30,780 --> 00:11:31,360
if you open it up,

363
00:11:31,360 --> 00:11:33,120
the contents are unreadable to us.

364
00:11:33,120 --> 00:11:34,760
In fact, it's a binary thing.

365
00:11:34,760 --> 00:11:36,220
Now this kind of thing

366
00:11:36,220 --> 00:11:37,940
is actually machine instructions.

367
00:11:37,940 --> 00:11:42,020
So these machine instructions,

368
00:11:42,020 --> 00:11:44,300
they are placed one by one in this AD-OUT,

369
00:11:44,300 --> 00:11:45,720
and loaded into memory.

370
00:11:45,720 --> 00:11:47,060
Then next,

371
00:11:47,060 --> 00:11:48,880
it starts being executed.

372
00:11:48,880 --> 00:11:50,680
Now at this point during execution,

373
00:11:50,680 --> 00:11:51,760
it involves what we just mentioned,

374
00:11:51,760 --> 00:11:52,520
this control unit,

375
00:11:52,520 --> 00:11:53,320
CU.

376
00:11:53,320 --> 00:11:54,940
This control unit

377
00:11:54,940 --> 00:11:56,780
will go into memory

378
00:11:56,780 --> 00:11:58,140
and fetch these machine instructions

379
00:11:58,140 --> 00:11:59,500
one by one.

380
00:11:59,500 --> 00:12:01,960
And the fetched ones

381
00:12:01,960 --> 00:12:02,860
This process,

382
00:12:02,860 --> 00:12:04,400
we can actually think of it as

383
00:12:04,400 --> 00:12:07,880
the whole process is

384
00:12:07,880 --> 00:12:08,720
basically three steps.

385
00:12:08,720 --> 00:12:10,580
The first step is called fetch,

386
00:12:10,580 --> 00:12:13,500
the second step is called decode,

387
00:12:13,500 --> 00:12:14,500
and the third step is execute.

388
00:12:14,500 --> 00:12:16,160
So the control unit,

389
00:12:16,160 --> 00:12:17,640
is really just doing these three things

390
00:12:17,640 --> 00:12:18,860
over and over again.

391
00:12:18,860 --> 00:12:21,940
Now, fetching,

392
00:12:21,940 --> 00:12:23,240
is called fetch in English,

393
00:12:23,240 --> 00:12:24,420
decode is called decode,

394
00:12:24,420 --> 00:12:25,700
and execution is called execute.

395
00:12:25,700 --> 00:12:26,960
Now, this fetch step,

396
00:12:26,960 --> 00:12:28,600
basically,

397
00:12:28,600 --> 00:12:29,920
takes the machine instruction,

398
00:12:29,920 --> 00:12:31,700
and fetches it from memory into where?

399
00:12:31,700 --> 00:12:34,760
It actually fetches it into a register.

400
00:12:34,760 --> 00:12:38,260
Into a register.

401
00:12:38,260 --> 00:12:39,440
That code,

402
00:12:39,440 --> 00:12:40,640
basically, we have to translate it.

403
00:12:40,640 --> 00:12:41,580
Because once it's fetched in,

404
00:12:41,580 --> 00:12:42,900
this 0101 computer

405
00:12:42,900 --> 00:12:43,680
has no idea what it means.

406
00:12:43,680 --> 00:12:45,400
It definitely has to translate it,

407
00:12:45,400 --> 00:12:47,120
translate it into an action it can recognize.

408
00:12:47,120 --> 00:12:48,500
Then, the third step,

409
00:12:48,500 --> 00:12:50,020
only then can it execute it,

410
00:12:50,020 --> 00:12:50,940
right?

411
00:12:50,940 --> 00:12:52,660
These are all fundamentals.

412
00:12:52,660 --> 00:12:55,200
If you don't remember them very clearly,

413
00:12:55,200 --> 00:12:57,420
let's quickly go over them again.

414
00:12:57,420 --> 00:12:58,800
So this,

415
00:12:58,800 --> 00:13:01,760
just does this over and over, one instruction at a time.

416
00:13:01,760 --> 00:13:07,560
And this process,

417
00:13:07,560 --> 00:13:08,680
is when the program starts executing.

418
00:13:08,680 --> 00:13:12,500
But some of you might ask,

419
00:13:12,500 --> 00:13:13,860
regarding this execution,

420
00:13:13,860 --> 00:13:15,360
for example, some students say,

421
00:13:15,360 --> 00:13:18,200
How is it that

422
00:13:18,200 --> 00:13:20,860
it's as if someone is pushing it to do this?

423
00:13:20,860 --> 00:13:23,640
So who is pushing it to do this?

424
00:13:23,640 --> 00:13:25,360
Now this brings us to,

425
00:13:25,360 --> 00:13:27,020
when we were just talking about

426
00:13:27,020 --> 00:13:29,260
the composition of a computer,

427
00:13:29,260 --> 00:13:30,740
there was one part I forgot to mention.

428
00:13:30,740 --> 00:13:32,320
That is, there is actually one important

429
00:13:32,320 --> 00:13:33,760
one important thing in a computer,

430
00:13:33,760 --> 00:13:35,180
called a crystal oscillator.

431
00:13:35,180 --> 00:13:36,400
It is also a piece of hardware,

432
00:13:36,400 --> 00:13:37,300
and this hardware,

433
00:13:37,300 --> 00:13:38,460
once power is turned on,

434
00:13:38,460 --> 00:13:40,840
it produces things like pulses,

435
00:13:40,840 --> 00:13:42,260
at a fixed frequency,

436
00:13:42,260 --> 00:13:44,260
to drive our processor

437
00:13:44,260 --> 00:13:46,620
to do that repeated cycle

438
00:13:46,620 --> 00:13:47,900
of fetching,

439
00:13:47,900 --> 00:13:49,300
decoding, and executing instructions,

440
00:13:49,300 --> 00:13:49,980
right?

441
00:13:49,980 --> 00:13:52,100
When you buy a computer or a phone,

442
00:13:52,100 --> 00:13:54,180
you often ask the salesperson,

443
00:13:54,180 --> 00:13:56,800
"What's the clock speed of this processor?"

444
00:13:56,800 --> 00:13:58,420
You get what I mean, right?

445
00:13:58,420 --> 00:14:00,520
The faster the clock frequency,

446
00:14:00,520 --> 00:14:03,280
the speed is actually driven by this crystal oscillator thing,

447
00:14:03,280 --> 00:14:04,820
that drives it.

448
00:14:04,820 --> 00:14:06,840
So the higher the clock speed,

449
00:14:06,840 --> 00:14:08,360
the faster the computer computes.

450
00:14:08,360 --> 00:14:09,280
It's really just that simple.

451
00:14:09,280 --> 00:14:11,340
And sometimes it's not enough for us, so we say,

452
00:14:11,340 --> 00:14:12,800
"This frequency isn't enough, I want to overclock it."

453
00:14:12,800 --> 00:14:14,540
I don't know if any of you have done that these days.

454
00:14:14,540 --> 00:14:16,200
You buy a computer,

455
00:14:16,200 --> 00:14:18,160
say it's 2GHz,

456
00:14:18,160 --> 00:14:19,820
and you feel 2GHz isn't enough for gaming,

457
00:14:19,820 --> 00:14:21,660
so you can go and overclock it.

458
00:14:21,660 --> 00:14:22,380
That can be done.

459
00:14:22,380 --> 00:14:23,620
Probably not as common now,

460
00:14:23,620 --> 00:14:24,300
but in the past,

461
00:14:24,300 --> 00:14:25,980
When you bought a computer

462
00:14:25,980 --> 00:14:26,780
a lot of people would also

463
00:14:26,780 --> 00:14:27,520
take this

464
00:14:27,520 --> 00:14:29,800
whether they could modify their own computer

465
00:14:29,800 --> 00:14:31,240
and overclock it

466
00:14:31,240 --> 00:14:32,320
basically raise its frequency

467
00:14:32,320 --> 00:14:34,360
as a way of showing off their skills

468
00:14:34,360 --> 00:14:36,740
I don't know if any of you still do this now

469
00:14:36,740 --> 00:14:38,520
Back then, I spent the same amount of money

470
00:14:38,520 --> 00:14:40,060
and bought the same computer

471
00:14:40,060 --> 00:14:41,840
because when it left the factory,

472
00:14:41,840 --> 00:14:42,580
its frequency was fixed

473
00:14:42,580 --> 00:14:45,220
Now, some of those enthusiasts

474
00:14:45,220 --> 00:14:46,640
who were more technically skilled

475
00:14:46,640 --> 00:14:48,500
would go into the settings on that CPU,

476
00:14:48,500 --> 00:14:49,220
make some changes,

477
00:14:49,220 --> 00:14:50,340
and raise its frequency

478
00:14:50,340 --> 00:14:52,060
so the CPU would run faster, right?

479
00:14:52,060 --> 00:14:53,660
And that made them very happy

480
00:14:53,660 --> 00:14:54,720
But doing this...

481
00:14:54,720 --> 00:14:56,080
Actually, it's not all that great either

482
00:14:56,080 --> 00:14:56,860
because the frequency is high

483
00:14:56,860 --> 00:14:58,560
and if it gets too high, the heat can get pretty intense too

484
00:14:58,560 --> 00:14:59,280
Sometimes if things go wrong,

485
00:14:59,280 --> 00:15:00,800
the computer just burns out.

486
00:15:00,800 --> 00:15:02,720
So it's a bit of a gamble for us.

487
00:15:02,720 --> 00:15:05,220
But from this we now know,

488
00:15:05,220 --> 00:15:05,880
that inside a computer,

489
00:15:05,880 --> 00:15:07,300
there's actually this precision thing going on.

490
00:15:07,300 --> 00:15:10,760
So overall,

